  "text": "The user wants a Python function that merges overlapping intervals, along with an explanation of its correctness and time complexity. This is a classic algorithm problem (LeetCode 56 \"Merge Intervals\"). Let me think carefully about how to write a clean, well-documented solution and provide a rigorous but accessible explanation.\n\n**Problem statement:**\nGiven a collection of intervals, each represented as a pair [start, end], merge all overlapping intervals so that the result covers exactly the same region of the line but with no overlapping intervals (and typically, no adjacent intervals that could be merged — i.e., the result should be a minimal set of disjoint intervals whose union equals the union of the input). We should also decide how to handle touching intervals like [1, 4] and [4, 5] — the standard convention is that they overlap (share the point 4), so they should be merged into [1, 5]. I should state this assumption explicitly.\n\n**Standard approach (sort-then-sweep):**\n\n1. Sort the intervals by their start value (and by end value as a tiebreaker, though it's not strictly necessary for correctness if we handle merging carefully).\n2. Initialize the result with the first interval (or process iteratively).\n3. Iterate",
